          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is the difference between E11 and E11.9, and when is each the right thing to quote?",
          "answer": "E11 and E11.9 are codes used in the ICD-10 classification to represent type 2 diabetes and specific aspects of it.\n\n- **E11** is a broader category that encompasses all instances of type 2 diabetes with various complications. It is important to note that E11 is never used as a standalone code; it always requires additional characters to specify the type of complication, such as kidney or ocular involvement (source: \"Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories\").\n\n- **E11.9**, on the other hand, is used specifically for cases of type 2 diabetes that are documented without any complications. This code is often quoted in documentation to reflect a diagnosis where no further complications are noted (source: \"Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories\").\n\nIn summary, use **E11** when there are complications related to type 2 diabetes, and use **E11.9** when diabetes is present without complications.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
